WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.799 --> 00:00:05.310
Disney's one of the biggest film studios on
the planet, and it’s no secret that a role

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:09.230
in one of their family-friendly blockbusters
can make an actor's career.

00:00:09.230 --> 00:00:13.210
However, not everyone's eager to work with
the Mouse House, and there are a number of

00:00:13.210 --> 00:00:18.689
stars who have actually refused major roles
in big Disney movies and shows.

00:00:18.689 --> 00:00:20.150
Anne Hathaway

00:00:20.150 --> 00:00:24.630
Although she did end up in the movie, Anne
Hathaway actually had the opportunity to take

00:00:24.630 --> 00:00:27.529
the lead role in Tim Burton's Alice in Wonderland.

00:00:27.529 --> 00:00:32.119
The actress turned it down because she felt
she had already played a very similar "princess-in-a-dress"

00:00:32.119 --> 00:00:34.170
type role in The Princess Diaries.

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:38.879
However, Hathaway told the studio she'd be
willing to play the White Queen instead — and

00:00:38.879 --> 00:00:40.179
that’s what she did.

00:00:40.179 --> 00:00:44.641
It ended up being a good decision, as Hathaway
took home some awards for her turn as The

00:00:44.641 --> 00:00:49.410
White Queen and it opened up the door for
newcomer Mia Wasikowska to take on the role

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:50.820
of Alice.

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:52.390
Warren Beatty

00:00:52.390 --> 00:00:57.300
Gilbert Gottfried’s Iago provided the comic
relief in Disney's Aladdin, always chiming

00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:01.339
in with a squawk or a screech to lighten the
mood during some of the film’s more intense

00:01:01.339 --> 00:01:02.339
moments.

00:01:02.339 --> 00:01:04.760
"Oh Jafar, you're too kind.

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:05.900
I'm embarrassed."

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:10.830
However, according to the actor, he almost
didn't get the chance to play the iconic role.

00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:15.970
Gottfried revealed in his memoir that in order
to land the part he had to beat out Joe Pesci

00:01:15.970 --> 00:01:16.970
and Danny DeVito.

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:21.790
Apparently the role was actually first offered
to the star of the 2017 Oscars Best Picture

00:01:21.790 --> 00:01:23.820
mix-up, Warren Beatty.

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:28.280
Beatty reportedly turned the role down because
playing a parrot in the desert felt too similar

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:30.340
to his role from Ishtar.

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:34.350
Just imagine how different the film would
have been without Gottfried’s iconic squawk.

00:01:34.350 --> 00:01:35.840
"My wits end."

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:37.160
"Wit's end!"

00:01:38.420 --> 00:01:39.420
Billy Crystal

00:01:39.430 --> 00:01:44.540
Toy Story is one of the most iconic films
of all time, with a 100 percent critical rating

00:01:44.540 --> 00:01:46.070
on Rotten Tomatoes.

00:01:46.070 --> 00:01:50.470
While Tim Allen's work as Buzz Lightyear is
classic, it turns out that the character was

00:01:50.470 --> 00:01:52.980
nearly brought to life by a different actor.

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:57.310
Billy Crystal, who would become famous with
Disney-Pixar fans for voicing Mike Wazowski

00:01:57.310 --> 00:02:02.070
in Monsters, Inc. was offered the part in
the early planning stages of the film.

00:02:02.070 --> 00:02:06.940
Apparently Pixar was so eager to get him to
sign on that the studio actually animated

00:02:06.940 --> 00:02:11.110
an early version of Buzz Lightyear with some
dialogue from When Harry Met Sally.

00:02:11.110 --> 00:02:12.960
The result is...interesting:

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:16.170
"Happy everyone is in love, and that's wonderful.

00:02:16.170 --> 00:02:20.570
But you gotta know, that some day, believe
it or not, you'll go 15 rounds over who's

00:02:20.570 --> 00:02:22.480
going to get this coffee table."

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:26.650
As we all know now, Crystal turned it down,
and Tim Allen got the part.

00:02:26.650 --> 00:02:31.700
Interestingly, while Crystal has had a great
career, he told ABC News in 2001 that passing

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:34.300
on Buzz is actually something he regrets.

00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:39.180
Interestingly, the Pixar movie Crystal did
appear in almost had a totally different costar,

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:39.740
too...

00:02:40.820 --> 00:02:42.220
Bill Murray

00:02:42.220 --> 00:02:47.360
In 2001, Monsters, Inc. became an instant
classic for Disney and Pixar fans.

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:52.380
The film won an Oscar and earned further accolades
for each of its two main leads, Billy Crystal

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:53.620
and John Goodman.

00:02:53.620 --> 00:02:58.400
But Goodman, who famously voiced Sulley, almost
didn't get the part.

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:02.459
Director Pete Docter's top choice for the
part was actually legendary comedian Bill

00:03:02.459 --> 00:03:03.459
Murray.

00:03:03.459 --> 00:03:08.010
The actor screen-tested for the part and impressed
Docter but when he called to offer him the

00:03:08.010 --> 00:03:10.790
part, he couldn't get Murray to pick up the
phone.

00:03:10.790 --> 00:03:15.421
The director of another Murray movie, St.
Vincent, explained just how difficult it is

00:03:15.421 --> 00:03:18.150
to track the guy down, just to offer him work:

00:03:18.150 --> 00:03:21.360
"The Job of Bill Murray is finding Bill Murray,
which everyone knows.

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:25.069
One you find Bill Murray, then the job kinda
goes downhill."

00:03:25.069 --> 00:03:26.069
 

00:03:26.069 --> 00:03:29.740
Murray is notoriously hard to reach so it’s
still unclear if this was his way of saying

00:03:29.740 --> 00:03:32.970
he wasn’t interested or if it was just a
miscommunication.

00:03:32.970 --> 00:03:37.310
Either way, you won’t hear any complaints
about it from John Goodman.

00:03:37.310 --> 00:03:39.250
Leonardo DiCaprio

00:03:39.250 --> 00:03:43.310
Hocus Pocus may not have been the biggest
Disney film of all time, but the movie has

00:03:43.310 --> 00:03:47.950
a huge cult following and is still the subject
of many a Halloween movie marathon.

00:03:47.950 --> 00:03:52.310
The filmmakers were especially picky about
searching for their star, and they eventually

00:03:52.310 --> 00:03:56.590
narrowed it down to the then up-and-comer
Leonardo DiCaprio to play Max.

00:03:56.590 --> 00:04:00.959
Despite being offered some serious coin, DiCaprio
decided to turn down the part because he wanted

00:04:00.959 --> 00:04:04.150
to wait and see if he'd landed the role in
What's Eating Gilbert Grape.

00:04:04.150 --> 00:04:08.830
Though it was likely terrifying at the time,
Dicaprio has said that this as the best — and

00:04:08.830 --> 00:04:11.069
toughest — decision he has ever made.

00:04:11.069 --> 00:04:15.580
It definitely worked out for him, as Leo earned
his first Academy Award nomination for What’s

00:04:15.580 --> 00:04:18.959
Eating Gilbert Grape and has been an A-lister
ever since.

00:04:18.959 --> 00:04:25.349
Omri Katz, who eventually took the role of
Max in Hocus Pocus, stopped acting in 2002.

00:04:25.349 --> 00:04:26.930
Lily Tomlin

00:04:26.930 --> 00:04:31.130
While it's hard to imagine anything different
in the Pixar classic, The Incredibles, one

00:04:31.130 --> 00:04:34.520
of the film's signature characters was almost
voiced by someone else.

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:39.680
Edna Mode, the superhero stylist who really,
really hates capes, was voiced by director

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:41.460
Brad Bird in the final product.

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:42.520
"No capes!"

00:04:42.520 --> 00:04:44.110
"Now, go on.."

00:04:44.110 --> 00:04:46.219
However, that's not what he'd intended.

00:04:46.219 --> 00:04:50.490
Apparently, when Bird recorded the voice for
Edna, he was just filling in for actress Lily

00:04:50.490 --> 00:04:52.720
Tomlin, who was set to play the part.

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:56.919
However, when she heard Bird's take on the
character, she decided that it wasn't something

00:04:56.919 --> 00:04:57.949
she could improve on.

00:04:57.949 --> 00:05:01.659
You know you’ve made it in show business
when you can pass on a Disney movie paycheck

00:05:01.659 --> 00:05:03.449
and not bat an eye.

00:05:03.449 --> 00:05:04.880
Michael Douglas

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:11.720
In 2013, Frozen became a commercial and critical
success bringing in over $1.3 billion and

00:05:11.729 --> 00:05:13.530
winning two Academy Awards.

00:05:13.530 --> 00:05:18.059
The voice acting in the movie was great, but
the casting might have turned out differently.

00:05:18.059 --> 00:05:22.569
Michael Douglas told James Corden that he
turned down a part in the film, but couldn’t

00:05:22.569 --> 00:05:24.320
remember exactly which role.

00:05:24.820 --> 00:05:25.320
"Frozen."

00:05:25.740 --> 00:05:26.319
"No!

00:05:26.319 --> 00:05:28.250
You were wont to play Elsa"

00:05:28.250 --> 00:05:29.800
I don't remember."

00:05:30.050 --> 00:05:33.820
Although we don’t know for sure, our best
guess is that Douglas was offered either the

00:05:33.830 --> 00:05:36.529
Duke of Weselton, Hans or Kristoff.

00:05:36.529 --> 00:05:39.830
Although it would be funny, it's probably
doubtful that Douglas was ever in line to

00:05:39.830 --> 00:05:41.550
voice Olaf.

00:05:41.550 --> 00:05:42.550
Selena Gomez

00:05:42.550 --> 00:05:47.261
It’s no secret that for many years Selena
Gomez was one of the queens of the Disney

00:05:47.261 --> 00:05:48.440
channel network.

00:05:48.440 --> 00:05:52.800
What's surprising, though, is that Gomez actually
ended up turning down the lead role of Mitchie

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:57.740
in Camp Rock because she wanted to do more
conventional and serious acting before ultimately

00:05:57.740 --> 00:06:00.810
transitioning into a more music-focused career.

00:06:00.810 --> 00:06:05.900
Gomez also reportedly turned down an undisclosed
part in High School Musical 3: Senior Year

00:06:05.900 --> 00:06:07.449
for similar reasons.

00:06:07.449 --> 00:06:12.029
These days Selena is one of the most successful
popstars around, but we have to wonder just

00:06:12.029 --> 00:06:16.569
how much her serious acting role in Spring
Breakers really helped her cause...

00:06:16.569 --> 00:06:18.039
Jack Nicholson

00:06:18.039 --> 00:06:23.129
Hercules is an animated Disney classic, and
that’s in large parts thanks to James Woods'

00:06:23.129 --> 00:06:25.020
smooth-talking Hades.

00:06:25.020 --> 00:06:26.060
"Ladies.

00:06:26.060 --> 00:06:27.099
Please."

00:06:27.099 --> 00:06:31.139
"My fate is in your lovely hands."

00:06:31.139 --> 00:06:32.139
"Owww."

00:06:32.139 --> 00:06:36.759
However, the part was apparently written with
Jack Nicholson specifically in mind, and the

00:06:36.759 --> 00:06:39.330
studio tried their best to land him for the
role.

00:06:39.330 --> 00:06:43.559
While Jack liked the creative side of the
deal, he and Disney were unable to agree on

00:06:43.559 --> 00:06:47.169
the business end of things — so he ended
up walking away from the role.

00:06:47.169 --> 00:06:52.759
After testing actors like Robert Evans and
John Lithgow, the filmmakers landed on Woods,

00:06:52.759 --> 00:06:53.830
and the rest is history.

00:06:53.830 --> 00:06:58.189
Could it be his great Hercules performance
that prompted Seth Macfarlane’s strange

00:06:58.189 --> 00:06:59.789
obsession with James Woods?

00:06:59.789 --> 00:07:00.789
"Because…

00:07:00.789 --> 00:07:03.199
James Woods… has you."

00:07:03.820 --> 00:07:06.400
"And I have James Woods…"

00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:07.060
"Too!"

00:07:07.610 --> 00:07:08.600
Thanks for watching!

00:07:08.610 --> 00:07:11.520
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:13.949
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

